# Brief: login screen (US-19), screen only

## Goal
A login screen at the route /login in the Expo client. It looks right and behaves right, but nothing is real behind it. No server calls, no real accounts, no stored passwords.

## In scope
- One new screen: client/src/app/login.tsx (expo-router makes the file a route, so /login works with no extra setup).
- A small form component that the screen uses, for example client/src/components/LoginForm.tsx. It takes an onSubmit(email, password) prop and does not know what happens next.
- The screen passes a stub to onSubmit. For now the stub only shows a message like "Login is not connected yet".
- Tests, written before the code.

## Out of scope (please do not do these)
- Any fetch call, any server endpoint, any change to client/src/lib/api.ts or server/.
- Saving the email or password anywhere (no storage, no context, no logging). Do not console.log them.
- Authentication or authorization logic (US-20 and US-21 are separate).
- Linking to the screen from the other screens, and changes to SemesterProvider.

## What the screen has
- A title, an Email field, a Password field (hidden text), and a Log in button.
- Email field: autoCapitalize none, keyboardType email-address.
- Password field: secureTextEntry.
- Empty email or password: show a short error under the form and do not call onSubmit.
- Valid input: call onSubmit once with the two values.
- Use colours and spacing from client/src/theme.ts. Look at components/ServerStatus.tsx and components/SectionHeader.tsx for the style we use.

## Accessibility and test IDs
- Give every field and the button an accessibilityLabel.
- testIDs: login-email, login-password, login-submit, login-error.

## Tests (write them first, in client/src/__tests__/login.test.tsx)
Use @testing-library/react-native like the other test files there. Cover: the fields and button render; empty fields show the error and do not call onSubmit; filled fields call onSubmit once with the typed values; the password field hides its text. Look at estimate-hours.test.tsx for the pattern, including the expo-router mock.

## Running it
After PR 62 merges the app needs the server running. For a screen-only task you can skip the server: create client/.env.local containing EXPO_PUBLIC_USE_FIXTURE=1, then run npx expo start -c from client/. Open /login in the browser (npm run web) or Expo Go.